// # Part 4 — `call`, `apply`, `bind`

// ### 11. Difference between `call`, `apply`, and `bind`

// ```js
// fn.call(obj, 10, 20);

// fn.apply(obj, [10, 20]);

// const newFn = fn.bind(obj, 10, 20);
// newFn();
// ```

// ### `call`

// ```js
// fn.call(obj, 10, 20);
// ```

// * Executes immediately.
// * Sets `this` to `obj`.
// * Arguments are passed individually.

// ### `apply`

// ```js
// fn.apply(obj, [10, 20]);
// ```

// * Executes immediately.
// * Sets `this` to `obj`.
// * Arguments are supplied as an array/array-like collection.

// ### `bind`

// ```js
// const newFn = fn.bind(obj, 10, 20);
// ```

// * Does **not** execute immediately.
// * Returns a new function.
// * Pre-binds `this`.
// * Can also pre-fill arguments.

// Then:

// ```js
// newFn();
// ```

// executes it.

