// # Part 8 — Lexical Scope / Scope Chain / Execution Context / Closure

// ### 19. What is lexical scope?

// > Lexical scope means variable accessibility is determined by where the code is written.

// Example:

// ```js
// let x = 10;

// function test() {
//     console.log(x);
// }
// ```

// `test` can access `x` because of where it was defined.

// ---

// ### 20. What is an execution context?

// An execution context is the runtime environment created when JavaScript executes code.

// For:

// ```js
// function add(a, b) {
//     return a + b;
// }

// add(10, 20);
// ```

// JavaScript creates an execution context for `add`.

// Conceptually it contains information such as:

// ```text
// a = 10
// b = 20
// local variables
// this
// outer environment reference
// ```

// ---

// ### 21. What is the scope chain?

// The scope chain is the chain of environments JavaScript searches when resolving a variable.

// Example:

// ```js
// let a = 10;

// function outer() {
//     let b = 20;

//     function inner() {
//         let c = 30;

//         console.log(a);
//         console.log(b);
//         console.log(c);
//     }

//     inner();
// }
// ```

// Inside `inner`:

// ```text
// inner scope
//    ↓
// outer scope
//    ↓
// global scope
// ```

// ---

// ### 22. What is a closure?

// A closure occurs when a function retains access to variables from its lexical environment after the outer function has finished executing.

// ```js
// function outer() {
//     let x = 10;

//     return function inner() {
//         return x;
//     };
// }

// const fn = outer();

// console.log(fn());
// ```

// Even after `outer()` finishes, `inner` can still access:

// ```text
// x = 10
// ```

// ---

