// # 28. Do arrow functions have their own `arguments`?

// No.

// ```js
// const fn = () => {
//     console.log(arguments);
// };
// ```

// The arrow doesn't create its own `arguments`.

// If there is no outer `arguments`, accessing it can produce:

// ```text
// ReferenceError
// ```

// Use rest parameters instead:

// ```js
// const fn = (...args) => {
//     console.log(args);
// };

// fn(10, 20);
// ```

// Output:

// ```text
// [10, 20]
// ```

// ---

// # 29. Can arrow functions be used with `new`?

// No.

// ```js
// const User = (name) => {
//     this.name = name;
// };

// const user = new User("Bhavya");
// ```

// Result:

// ```text
// TypeError
// ```

// Arrow functions are **not constructible**.

// ---

// # 30. Do arrow functions have `prototype`?

// No.

// ```js
// const fn = () => {};

// console.log(fn.prototype);
// ```

// Output:

// ```text
// undefined
// ```

// Ordinary constructor-capable functions generally have a `prototype` property.

// ---

// # 31. Can `call()` change arrow-function `this`?

// No.

// ```js
// const obj = {
//     name: "Bhavya"
// };

// const fn = () => {
//     console.log(this.name);
// };

// fn.call(obj);
// ```

// `call(obj)` cannot make the arrow's `this` become `obj`.

// Why?

// Because arrow functions use lexical `this`.

// Same principle applies to:

// ```js
// apply()
// bind()
// ```

// They cannot override an arrow function's lexical `this`.

